{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,9]],"date-time":"2026-09-09T10:09:56Z","timestamp":1788948596546,"version":"build-2803163510"},"reference-count":45,"publisher":"Wiley","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,21]],"date-time":"2026-08-21T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1787270400000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0\/"},{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,21]],"date-time":"2026-08-21T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1787270400000},"content-version":"tdm","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/doi.wiley.com\/10.1002\/tdm_license_1.1"}],"content-domain":{"domain":["onlinelibrary.wiley.com"],"crossmark-restriction":true},"short-container-title":["Int J App Linguistics"],"abstract":"<jats:title>ABSTRACT<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    Self\u2010regulation (SR) plays a central role in instructed language learning, as it enables learners to set goals, monitor progress, and adapt learning behaviour through reflection. We investigate the contribution of two core self\u2010regulatory processes\u2014performance control (PC) and reflection (R)\u2014to attainment (A) in structure\u2010focused L2 English and L2 German courses. In the context of our study, these courses are taught to language majors and involve explicit, analytically oriented instruction in language structure, including phonology, morphology, syntax, and semantics, rather than skills\u2010focused language teaching. Addressing calls to move beyond self\u2010report measures, we drew on behavioural indicators of self\u2010regulation, including engagement in non\u2010graded Moodle activities and participation in structured reflection tasks. Data was collected longitudinally at four time points over one academic year from 119 learners enrolled in English L2\/German L3 (\n                    <jats:italic>n<\/jats:italic>\n                    = 70) and German L2\/English L3 (\n                    <jats:italic>n<\/jats:italic>\n                    = 49) programmes. Relationships among PC, R, and A were examined using longitudinal linear mixed\u2010effects models consistent with cyclical accounts of self\u2010regulated learning. Analyses supported a process\u2010consistent pattern in which R was associated with PC, while PC predicted A and R showed no direct association with A once PC was included in the model. Importantly, these relationships differed across L2 learning contexts, with PC showing a stronger association with A in L2 German than L2 English. The findings point to the value of linking structured reflection with behavioural engagement in low\u2010stakes practice, particularly in the L2 German context, rather than treating reflection as an isolated instructional target.\n                  <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1111\/ijal.70342","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,21]],"date-time":"2026-08-21T13:35:34Z","timestamp":1787319334000},"update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/crossmark_policy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Performance Control, Reflection, and Attainment in Instructed Language Learning: A Longitudinal Study of Self\u2010Regulation in L2 English and L2 German Courses"],"prefix":"10.1111","author":[{"given":"Jakub","family":"Przyby\u0142","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Adam Mickiewicz University  Pozna\u0144 Poland"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Agnieszka","family":"Po\u017alewicz","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Adam Mickiewicz University  Pozna\u0144 Poland"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Justyna","family":"Duch\u2010Adamczyk","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Adam Mickiewicz University  Pozna\u0144 Poland"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"311","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,21]]},"reference":[{"key":"e_1_2_10_2_1","doi-